### Branching Logic in C#

Conditional statements control the execution path based on boolean evaluations:

```csharp
if (condition1)
{
    // Code executed when condition1 is true
}
else if (condition2)
{
    // Code executed when condition1 is false and condition2 is true
}
else
{
    // Fallback when all conditions are false
}
```

#### Strict Type Safety
In C#, conditions **must** evaluate strictly to a `bool` type (`true` or `false`). Unlike C or C++, writing `if (count)` where `count` is an integer produces a compile-time error. Always write explicit relational comparisons: `if (count > 0)`.

